  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn anomalous trough will set up over the western United States\nthrough this weekend, centered over the Great Basin and Northern\nRockies by Sunday. As southeastern Arizona sits on the southern\nperiphery, dry conditions with gusty southwest winds will be the\nstory over the coming days along with the arrival of near to  \nslightly below normal temperatures by early next week. \n\nToday the combination of gusty winds and dry atmospheric\nmoisture will produce critical fire weather conditions with a\nRed Flag Warning in effect today and Sunday for much of the \narea. Southwesterly winds of 15-25 mph with gusts of 30-40 mph \nwill be common through the early evening and again on Sunday.\nThe dry air that has moved in has mostly cut off precipitation \nchances for southeastern Arizona, with lingering but modest \nmoisture still in southeastern Cochise county. Satellite derived\nmoisture depicts precipitable water values below but near 1 \ninch in SE Cochise, which should allow for a few isolated \nthunderstorms late this afternoon through the early evening. \nHowever the majority of this activity will be off to the south \nand east. No real signal for severe weather exists with this \nactivity and they should move quick enough in the active \nsouthwest/west flow to preclude a flooding threat, however a \nvery isolated strong wind gust can't be ruled out. \n\nWhile the upper trough will undergo a transition early next\nweek, southeastern Arizona will likely remain under a similar\npattern for several more days. This should bring at least near \ncritical fire weather conditions on Monday. This pattern will \nkeep high temperatures near normal to just below normal Sunday \nthrough mid-week. The forecast is dry the coming week with the \nwest/southwest flow expected, though some model members do try \nto allow moisture back into the area in the second half of the \nweek which may increase the forecast chances somewhat as we get \ncloser to this period. Better signals for moisture return and \nmonsoon activity continue to hold off until after July 4th, when\nlong range models start to build a western ridge in the \nsubsequent days.",
